Exercise 2.18 defines \(h\colon\mathbb{B}\to\mathbb{B}\) by \(h(0x)=00h(x)\) and \(h(1x)=10h(x)\) for every element \(x\), asks what \(h\) means, whether it is an isomorphism, and whether some \(k\) satisfies \(k\circ h=I_{\mathbb{B}}\) with \(k\) one-one. `hOut` answers the verbal question on finite strings: the empty string is fixed (`hOut_nil`), and a string starting with a bit \(b\) is sent to \(b\) followed by `false` followed by the doubled tail (`hOut_cons`), so each bit \(b\) becomes the block \(b0\), with `false` the zero bit of the two equations. `hOut_mono` says doubling preserves prefixes, and `hMap` is the approximable map relating an input cone to an output neighbourhood when the cone of the doubled prefix is contained in that neighbourhood; `toElementMap_hMap_cone` sends the principal cone of \(\sigma\) to the principal cone of `hOut σ`. `kOut` is the left inverse on prefixes: it is empty on the empty string and on a singleton (`kOut_nil`, `kOut_single`), and on a string that starts with two bits it keeps the first and continues on the remaining tail (`kOut_cons`), so a trailing unpaired bit is dropped. `kOut_hOut` is the induction that this reading undoes doubling, and `kMap_comp_hMap` turns the cone form of that identity into \(k\circ h=I_{\mathbb{B}}\) by extensionality of approximable relations. `hMap_not_surjective` shows that the principal cone of the string `false` followed by `true`, that is \(01\), is not a value of the element map: a preimage would equal its image under \(k\), and the two cone formulas would force the doubled string \(00\) to equal \(01\). `kMap_not_injective` sends both length-one principal cones to the principal cone of the empty string, so \(k\) collapses them. `example_218` is the conjunction of non-surjectivity of \(h\), the identity composite, and non-injectivity of \(k\). The left inverse makes \(h\) injective, so the failure to be an isomorphism is the failure of surjectivity. **Theorem:** yes — \(h\) inserts a zero after every bit, it is not an isomorphism, and \(k\) is a left inverse that is not one-one. **Proof:** yes — the definitional recurrence, the inductive cancellation `kOut_hOut`, the relational extensionality of the composite, and the two cone contradictions are the arguments above.
